    cout << 'x = ' << x << ', y = ' << y << endl;
    return 0;
}"

answer:
The issue with this code is that the swap function is using call-by-value instead of call-by-reference. This means that when the swap function is called, it creates local copies of the arguments passed. Thus, swapping the local copy doesn't affect the original values of x and y.

Here is the corrected C++ code that would swap the values using call-by-reference:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void swap(int& a, int& b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

int main() {
    int x = 10;
    int y = 20;
    swap(x, y);
    cout << "x = " << x << ", y = " << y << endl;
    return 0;
